 Φ(1.75) & =0.9599.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.5) & =1+Φ(1.25)-Φ(1.75) \\\\  & =1+0.8944-0.9599 \\\\  & =0.9345.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534445 17592186045417 true] [17592186421850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421846 13194139534445 17592186045417 true] [17592186421850 1069 "q-143300" 13194139534445 17592186045417 true] [17592186421850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534445 17592186045417 true] [17592186421850 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{36}∼N(4,{18}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $36$ from a normal population, calculate $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<2.07).$\n\n*Hint: Value tables of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2358\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2327\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2296\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2676\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2643\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2611\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3015\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2981\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2946\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th a